1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,908
This is Egypt.

2
00:00:00,908 --> 00:00:03,452
It's roughly the size of France and Spain

3
00:00:03,452 --> 00:00:04,300
put together.

4
00:00:04,650 --> 00:00:06,598
And it's home to more than a hundred

5
00:00:06,598 --> 00:00:07,440
million people.

6
00:00:07,790 --> 00:00:09,287
But almost all of them,

7
00:00:09,287 --> 00:00:10,971
about ninety-five percent,

8
00:00:10,971 --> 00:00:12,842
live on this thin green line,

9
00:00:12,842 --> 00:00:15,649
which covers only around five percent of the

10
00:00:15,649 --> 00:00:16,210
country.

11
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:19,235
So why would a hundred million people squeeze

12
00:00:19,235 --> 00:00:20,630
into such a tiny space?

13
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:25,157
By the end of this video,

14
00:00:25,157 --> 00:00:27,583
you'll see why this strip gave birth to

15
00:00:27,583 --> 00:00:29,887
one of the first great civilizations,

16
00:00:29,887 --> 00:00:32,374
how Egypt moved an entire temple to save

17
00:00:32,374 --> 00:00:32,616
it,

18
00:00:32,616 --> 00:00:34,739
and why the river behind it all is

19
00:00:34,739 --> 00:00:36,740
now at the center of a conflict.

20
00:00:37,090 --> 00:00:38,415
Take away the Nile,

21
00:00:38,415 --> 00:00:40,866
and Egypt is almost entirely desert.

22
00:00:40,866 --> 00:00:43,780
The Sahara covers nearly the whole country.

23
00:00:44,130 --> 00:00:45,667
And look at the borders.

24
00:00:45,667 --> 00:00:49,233
Egypt's western and southern borders are almost perfectly

25
00:00:49,233 --> 00:00:50,216
straight lines.

26
00:00:50,216 --> 00:00:53,536
They were drawn in agreements between colonial powers

27
00:00:53,536 --> 00:00:55,934
along lines of longitude and latitude,

28
00:00:55,934 --> 00:00:58,208
and they could be drawn with a ruler

29
00:00:58,208 --> 00:01:00,360
because almost nobody lived there.

30
00:01:00,710 --> 00:01:04,134
Cairo gets only around twenty-five millimeters of rain

31
00:01:04,134 --> 00:01:05,317
in an entire year.

32
00:01:05,317 --> 00:01:07,870
London gets more than twenty times that.

33
00:01:08,220 --> 00:01:10,839
So picture Egypt as one hundred squares of

34
00:01:10,839 --> 00:01:11,204
land.

35
00:01:11,204 --> 00:01:13,701
Only about five of them are green enough

36
00:01:13,701 --> 00:01:14,980
to live on and farm.

37
00:01:15,330 --> 00:01:16,732
Now picture the people.

38
00:01:16,732 --> 00:01:19,886
About ninety-five out of every hundred Egyptians live

39
00:01:19,886 --> 00:01:21,230
on those five squares.

40
00:01:21,580 --> 00:01:23,841
That makes the Nile Valley one of the

41
00:01:23,841 --> 00:01:26,161
most crowded farming regions on Earth,

42
00:01:26,161 --> 00:01:29,135
with roughly two thousand people for every square

43
00:01:29,135 --> 00:01:30,860
kilometer of habitable land.

44
00:01:33,810 --> 00:01:35,296
The answer is one river.

45
00:01:35,296 --> 00:01:35,891
The Nile,

46
00:01:35,891 --> 00:01:38,269
one of the longest rivers in the world,

47
00:01:38,269 --> 00:01:41,421
roughly six thousand six hundred kilometers from its

48
00:01:41,421 --> 00:01:43,680
farthest source to the Mediterranean.

49
00:01:44,030 --> 00:01:46,951
Whether it's the longest river on Earth depends

50
00:01:46,951 --> 00:01:47,924
on who you ask.

51
00:01:47,924 --> 00:01:50,358
Some measurements put the Amazon ahead.

52
00:01:50,358 --> 00:01:52,670
It all comes down to where you decide

53
00:01:52,670 --> 00:01:54,070
a river really begins.

54
00:01:54,420 --> 00:01:56,293
And here's the strange part.

55
00:01:56,293 --> 00:01:59,201
Most of Egypt's water doesn't fall in Egypt.

56
00:01:59,201 --> 00:02:02,237
It falls thousands of kilometers to the south,

57
00:02:02,237 --> 00:02:05,144
in the highlands of Ethiopia and around Lake

58
00:02:05,144 --> 00:02:05,790
Victoria.

59
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:07,036
Every summer,

60
00:02:07,036 --> 00:02:09,852
monsoon rains soak the Ethiopian Highlands.

61
00:02:09,852 --> 00:02:12,220
The water rushes down the Blue Nile,

62
00:02:12,220 --> 00:02:14,396
joins the White Nile at Khartoum,

63
00:02:14,396 --> 00:02:15,036
in Sudan,

64
00:02:15,036 --> 00:02:16,124
and flows north,

65
00:02:16,124 --> 00:02:17,980
straight through the Sahara.

66
00:02:18,330 --> 00:02:21,080
That's why the Nile does something that looks

67
00:02:21,080 --> 00:02:22,335
impossible on a map.

68
00:02:22,335 --> 00:02:25,085
It crosses well over a thousand kilometers of

69
00:02:25,085 --> 00:02:27,955
desert without a single major river joining it,

70
00:02:27,955 --> 00:02:29,330
and without drying up.

71
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:30,726
It isn't easy.

72
00:02:30,726 --> 00:02:32,122
In the desert heat,

73
00:02:32,122 --> 00:02:35,191
the river loses an enormous amount of water

74
00:02:35,191 --> 00:02:37,284
to evaporation along the way.

75
00:02:37,284 --> 00:02:39,586
Only a river fed by a huge rainy

76
00:02:39,586 --> 00:02:43,422
region thousands of kilometers away could survive this

77
00:02:43,422 --> 00:02:44,050
journey.

78
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:46,094
And for thousands of years,

79
00:02:46,094 --> 00:02:48,817
the river did something even more important.

80
00:02:48,817 --> 00:02:49,664
Every summer,

81
00:02:49,664 --> 00:02:50,390
it flooded.

82
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:55,279
From around July to September,

83
00:02:55,279 --> 00:02:58,094
the river rose and spread across the valley.

84
00:02:58,094 --> 00:02:59,845
When the water pulled back,

85
00:02:59,845 --> 00:03:01,847
it left behind a layer of dark,

86
00:03:01,847 --> 00:03:02,660
fertile mud.

87
00:03:03,010 --> 00:03:05,941
Ancient Egyptians called their country Kemet,

88
00:03:05,941 --> 00:03:06,961
the black land,

89
00:03:06,961 --> 00:03:07,980
after that mud.

90
00:03:07,980 --> 00:03:10,147
The desert around it was Deshret,

91
00:03:10,147 --> 00:03:11,039
the red land.

92
00:03:11,039 --> 00:03:12,186
Life on one side.

93
00:03:12,186 --> 00:03:13,460
Death on the other.

94
00:03:13,810 --> 00:03:16,385
Their whole calendar followed the river.

95
00:03:16,385 --> 00:03:17,327
Three seasons:

96
00:03:17,327 --> 00:03:17,766
Akhet,

97
00:03:17,766 --> 00:03:18,457
the flood.

98
00:03:18,457 --> 00:03:18,897
Peret,

99
00:03:18,897 --> 00:03:20,153
the growing season.

100
00:03:20,153 --> 00:03:20,844
And Shemu,

101
00:03:20,844 --> 00:03:21,660
the harvest.

102
00:03:22,010 --> 00:03:23,705
They even built nilometers,

103
00:03:23,705 --> 00:03:26,733
stone staircases and wells that measured how high

104
00:03:26,733 --> 00:03:27,702
the flood rose.

105
00:03:27,702 --> 00:03:29,034
The higher the water,

106
00:03:29,034 --> 00:03:30,487
the bigger the harvest,

107
00:03:30,487 --> 00:03:32,970
and the more tax farmers paid that year.

108
00:03:33,320 --> 00:03:36,272
The Greek historian Herodotus summed it up about

109
00:03:36,272 --> 00:03:38,380
two and a half thousand years ago.

110
00:03:38,380 --> 00:03:38,801
Egypt,

111
00:03:38,801 --> 00:03:39,404
he wrote,

112
00:03:39,404 --> 00:03:40,970
is the gift of the river.

113
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:43,933
Now look where the great monuments are.

114
00:03:43,933 --> 00:03:45,370
The pyramids of Giza.

115
00:03:45,370 --> 00:03:46,807
The temples of Luxor.

116
00:03:46,807 --> 00:03:47,264
Aswan.

117
00:03:47,264 --> 00:03:49,681
Every one of them sits right next to

118
00:03:49,681 --> 00:03:50,400
the river.

119
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:52,914
The Nile was also Egypt's highway,

120
00:03:52,914 --> 00:03:54,212
with a clever trick.

121
00:03:54,212 --> 00:03:55,758
The current flows north,

122
00:03:55,758 --> 00:03:58,416
but the wind usually blows from the north.

123
00:03:58,416 --> 00:04:01,260
So boats drifted downstream with the current,

124
00:04:01,260 --> 00:04:03,794
then raised their sails and let the wind

125
00:04:03,794 --> 00:04:05,340
push them back upstream.

126
00:04:05,690 --> 00:04:07,684
Even their writing reflects it.

127
00:04:07,684 --> 00:04:08,681
In hieroglyphs,

128
00:04:08,681 --> 00:04:11,298
the sign for traveling south shows a boat

129
00:04:11,298 --> 00:04:12,419
with its sail up.

130
00:04:12,419 --> 00:04:13,478
Traveling north,

131
00:04:13,478 --> 00:04:14,600
the sail is down.

132
00:04:14,950 --> 00:04:17,142
And the flood may even help explain the

133
00:04:17,142 --> 00:04:17,689
pyramids.

134
00:04:17,689 --> 00:04:20,429
Many historians think that during the months when

135
00:04:20,429 --> 00:04:21,963
the fields were underwater,

136
00:04:21,963 --> 00:04:24,319
farmers were free to work on the pharaohs'

137
00:04:24,319 --> 00:04:25,360
building projects.

138
00:04:25,710 --> 00:04:27,597
Reliable food from the river,

139
00:04:27,597 --> 00:04:30,428
and deserts on every side that kept invaders

140
00:04:30,428 --> 00:04:30,805
away.

141
00:04:30,805 --> 00:04:33,385
That combination is a big reason why one

142
00:04:33,385 --> 00:04:36,467
of the world's first great states appeared here,

143
00:04:36,467 --> 00:04:38,480
around five thousand years ago.

144
00:04:38,830 --> 00:04:40,233
If you're enjoying this,

145
00:04:40,233 --> 00:04:40,851
subscribe.

146
00:04:40,851 --> 00:04:43,040
Every week we find the story hidden in

147
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:43,770
another map.

148
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:48,879
But the flood was never predictable.

149
00:04:48,879 --> 00:04:50,689
Too little water meant hunger.

150
00:04:50,689 --> 00:04:53,140
Too much could wash entire villages away.

151
00:04:53,490 --> 00:04:55,230
So in the twentieth century,

152
00:04:55,230 --> 00:04:57,870
Egypt decided to control the river once and

153
00:04:57,870 --> 00:04:58,410
for all.

154
00:04:58,760 --> 00:04:59,411
In 1970,

155
00:04:59,411 --> 00:05:01,871
the Aswan High Dam was completed.

156
00:05:01,871 --> 00:05:03,896
Behind it grew Lake Nasser,

157
00:05:03,896 --> 00:05:07,007
one of the largest artificial lakes in the

158
00:05:07,007 --> 00:05:07,513
world,

159
00:05:07,513 --> 00:05:10,190
around five hundred kilometers long.

160
00:05:10,540 --> 00:05:13,412
The new lake was going to drown ancient

161
00:05:13,412 --> 00:05:14,201
monuments.

162
00:05:14,201 --> 00:05:15,422
So in the 1960s,

163
00:05:15,422 --> 00:05:18,868
an international campaign led by UNESCO cut the

164
00:05:18,868 --> 00:05:21,955
giant temples of Abu Simbel into more than

165
00:05:21,955 --> 00:05:25,186
a thousand blocks and rebuilt them on higher

166
00:05:25,186 --> 00:05:25,760
ground,

167
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:27,770
about sixty-five meters up.

168
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:30,360
It wasn't the first dam at Aswan.

169
00:05:30,360 --> 00:05:32,732
A smaller one had been built by the

170
00:05:32,732 --> 00:05:33,852
British in 1902.

171
00:05:33,852 --> 00:05:36,224
But the new dam was on a completely

172
00:05:36,224 --> 00:05:37,344
different scale,

173
00:05:37,344 --> 00:05:39,320
and it changed Egypt forever.

174
00:05:39,670 --> 00:05:41,294
The dam ended the floods.

175
00:05:41,294 --> 00:05:43,230
It stored water for dry years,

176
00:05:43,230 --> 00:05:46,040
and it produced huge amounts of electricity.

177
00:05:46,390 --> 00:05:47,854
But there was a price.

178
00:05:47,854 --> 00:05:50,655
The fertile mud now gets trapped behind the

179
00:05:50,655 --> 00:05:50,973
dam.

180
00:05:50,973 --> 00:05:53,328
Farmers rely on fertilizers instead,

181
00:05:53,328 --> 00:05:55,111
and without fresh sediment,

182
00:05:55,111 --> 00:05:58,039
parts of the Nile Delta are slowly retreating

183
00:05:58,039 --> 00:05:58,930
into the sea.

184
00:06:01,880 --> 00:06:02,286
Today,

185
00:06:02,286 --> 00:06:04,429
the delta and the valley hold one of

186
00:06:04,429 --> 00:06:06,110
the biggest cities on Earth.

187
00:06:06,110 --> 00:06:08,543
Greater Cairo is home to more than twenty

188
00:06:08,543 --> 00:06:09,470
million people.

189
00:06:09,820 --> 00:06:13,559
There's one more thin strip where Egyptians cluster:

190
00:06:13,559 --> 00:06:15,111
along the Suez Canal.

191
00:06:15,111 --> 00:06:16,240
Opened in 1869,

192
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:19,274
it links the Mediterranean to the Red Sea,

193
00:06:19,274 --> 00:06:21,884
and a big share of the world's trade

194
00:06:21,884 --> 00:06:23,224
passes through it,

195
00:06:23,224 --> 00:06:26,540
bringing Egypt billions of dollars every year.

196
00:06:26,890 --> 00:06:29,077
But the delta faces a new threat.

197
00:06:29,077 --> 00:06:31,136
Much of it lies only a meter or

198
00:06:31,136 --> 00:06:32,358
two above the sea,

199
00:06:32,358 --> 00:06:34,674
and as sea levels rise and the land

200
00:06:34,674 --> 00:06:35,575
slowly sinks,

201
00:06:35,575 --> 00:06:38,405
salt water is creeping into some of Egypt's

202
00:06:38,405 --> 00:06:39,370
best farmland.

203
00:06:39,720 --> 00:06:41,473
And Egypt keeps growing.

204
00:06:41,473 --> 00:06:45,330
Its population has multiplied roughly five times since

205
00:06:45,330 --> 00:06:45,751
1950,

206
00:06:45,751 --> 00:06:48,416
and almost all of that growth has had

207
00:06:48,416 --> 00:06:50,870
to fit into the same narrow strip.

208
00:06:51,220 --> 00:06:54,055
And the river itself is under pressure.

209
00:06:54,055 --> 00:06:54,764
Upstream,

210
00:06:54,764 --> 00:06:58,662
Ethiopia has built Africa's largest hydroelectric dam,

211
00:06:58,662 --> 00:07:01,284
the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam,

212
00:07:01,284 --> 00:07:02,560
on the Blue Nile.

213
00:07:02,910 --> 00:07:03,600
For Egypt,

214
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:06,297
which gets more than ninety percent of its

215
00:07:06,297 --> 00:07:07,991
fresh water from the Nile,

216
00:07:07,991 --> 00:07:09,308
that's a huge worry.

217
00:07:09,308 --> 00:07:10,186
For Ethiopia,

218
00:07:10,186 --> 00:07:13,323
the dam means electricity for millions of people.

219
00:07:13,323 --> 00:07:15,895
Both countries see the river as a matter

220
00:07:15,895 --> 00:07:16,710
of survival.

221
00:07:17,060 --> 00:07:19,353
Egypt is also trying to break out of

222
00:07:19,353 --> 00:07:20,096
the valley.

223
00:07:20,096 --> 00:07:21,026
East of Cairo,

224
00:07:21,026 --> 00:07:23,629
it's building a brand new capital city in

225
00:07:23,629 --> 00:07:24,372
the desert,

226
00:07:24,372 --> 00:07:26,851
and it's pumping Nile water to farm new

227
00:07:26,851 --> 00:07:28,400
land far from the river.

228
00:07:28,750 --> 00:07:31,774
So why do ninety-five percent of Egyptians live

229
00:07:31,774 --> 00:07:33,600
on five percent of the land?

230
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:36,309
Because in a country where it almost never

231
00:07:36,309 --> 00:07:36,750
rains,

232
00:07:36,750 --> 00:07:38,955
the Nile is the only water you can

233
00:07:38,955 --> 00:07:39,585
count on.

234
00:07:39,585 --> 00:07:41,159
For five thousand years,

235
00:07:41,159 --> 00:07:42,734
wherever the river goes,

236
00:07:42,734 --> 00:07:43,490
Egypt goes.

237
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:49,327
And Egypt isn't the only country shaped by

238
00:07:49,327 --> 00:07:52,261
where people can live.

239
00:07:52,261 --> 00:07:53,027
Next,

240
00:07:53,027 --> 00:07:58,896
we'll find out why most Canadians live within

241
00:07:58,896 --> 00:08:03,490
a short drive of the United States.
